EXTRA = [
    {"after": "physiology", "id": "membrane", "nav": "Membrane transport", "tag": "Physiology in depth", "h2": "The membrane in depth: why the same bag behaves differently in different people",
     "html": """
<p><strong>The capillary wall is the real filter.</strong> We call the peritoneum the membrane. The barrier that decides what crosses, though, is the wall of the small blood vessels running through it. Picture that wall as a sieve with two kinds of opening. Gaps between the lining cells let water and small solutes pass together: urea, creatinine, potassium, and glucose. Much smaller channels through the cells let only water pass.</p>
<p>The picture has a limit. A sieve has fixed holes. The capillary wall is alive, and the number of vessels in contact with the fluid differs from person to person and changes over the years. Two consequences follow. Small solutes move by diffusion, quickly at first and then more slowly as the fluid comes to resemble blood. Water moves only while the solution is more concentrated than blood. Glucose is itself a small solute, so it leaks back into the blood through the same gaps. The pull on water is strongest in the first hour or two and then fades. If the dwell runs long after the glucose gradient is gone, fluid is reabsorbed and the patient drains less than was filled.</p>
<p>That is why glucose strength and dwell length are prescribed as a pair. Glucose solutions come in three strengths, commonly labeled 1.5%, 2.5%, and 4.25% dextrose. A stronger bag removes more water. It also delivers more glucose to the patient and, over years, to the membrane. The prescriber therefore uses the weakest strength that keeps the patient at target weight.</p>
<p><strong>Transport type.</strong> The peritoneal equilibration test measures how fast this particular membrane works. A standard bag dwells for 4 hours. Samples of fluid and blood show how fast creatinine has entered the fluid and how fast glucose has left it. The result places the patient on a scale from fast to slow transport and guides the prescription.[[brown2020]]</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>Fast transporter</th><th>Slow transporter</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Small solutes</td><td>Equilibrate quickly</td><td>Equilibrate slowly</td></tr>
<tr><td>Glucose gradient</td><td>Lost early, so water removal is poor on long dwells</td><td>Kept longer, so water removal is sustained</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typical problem</td><td>Fluid overload, low drain volumes after long dwells</td><td>Solute removal that is too low if dwells are short</td></tr>
<tr><td>Prescription that suits</td><td>Short dwells, often automated overnight therapy; icodextrin for the long dwell</td><td>Longer dwells with adequate volume, as in manual daytime exchanges</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Icodextrin.</strong> Icodextrin is a large starch-derived molecule. It is absorbed slowly, so it keeps drawing water for many hours. It is used once a day for the long dwell: overnight in CAPD, daytime in APD. Two nursing points matter. First, it is broken down to maltose, and some bedside glucose meters and strips read maltose as glucose. The result is falsely high, and insulin given for it can cause severe hypoglycemia. Test a patient on icodextrin with a glucose-specific method, at home and in hospital, and make sure the chart carries that alert. Second, report a new rash after it is started.</p>
<p><strong>Ultrafiltration failure.</strong> Some patients stay overloaded despite strong bags. Ask the practical questions first. Is salt and fluid intake high? Are exchanges being missed? Is there a drainage problem or a leak? Is urine volume falling? Is high blood sugar flattening the gradient? True membrane failure usually follows years of therapy or repeated peritonitis. By the standard definition, it is less than 400 mL of net fluid removal after a 4-hour dwell with the strongest glucose solution. Your contribution is the record that shows the trend: daily weights, bag strengths used, and drain volumes.</p>
<p><strong>Remaining kidney function.</strong> Urine from the patient&rsquo;s own kidneys removes salt, water, and larger waste molecules all day. In PD, keeping it is associated with better survival and easier fluid control.[[brown2020]] The association is observational, and patients who keep their urine may be healthier in other ways. Protecting it is still a daily nursing task, because it costs little. Watch for over-removal of fluid and dehydration, including during diarrhea or vomiting. Question nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. Make sure anyone prescribing an aminoglycoside antibiotic or ordering a contrast study knows the patient still passes urine. Ask about urine volume at every visit. A fall is a reason to review the prescription, because the dialysis must now do more.</p>
<p><strong>When the record shows less fluid out than in, ask four questions: the bag, the dwell, the drain, and the diet.</strong></p>
"""},
    {"after": "membrane", "id": "catheter", "nav": "Catheter and start", "tag": "Clinical application", "h2": "The catheter, the break-in period, and an urgent start",
     "html": """
<p>The PD catheter is a soft silicone tube in three parts. Inside the abdomen, a segment with many side holes lies low in the pelvis. A middle segment runs in a tunnel under the skin. The external segment ends in a connector and a transfer set. Most catheters carry two felt cuffs. The deep cuff sits in the muscle of the abdominal wall, and the superficial cuff sits under the skin a short distance from the exit site. Over several weeks tissue grows into the cuffs. That anchors the catheter and seals the track, against fluid leaking out and against organisms tracking in. The exit site is planned to point downward or sideways, away from the belt line and skin folds, where the patient can see and reach it.</p>
<p><strong>Break-in.</strong> Filling the abdomen raises the pressure inside it. If full volumes are used before tissue has healed around the cuffs, fluid can leak along the catheter. A leak delays healing and invites infection. For a planned start, therapy is usually delayed about two weeks after insertion, and the catheter is flushed on the unit&rsquo;s schedule meanwhile. When dialysis cannot wait, an <strong>urgent start</strong> is possible. Small fill volumes are used with the patient lying down, because pressure in the abdomen is lowest in that position. Volumes are increased gradually, and the abdomen is left empty when the patient is up and walking, as the program directs.</p>
<p><strong>Early care after insertion</strong> follows the program protocol. The principles do not change:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Keep the dressing dry and intact.</strong> It is usually left undisturbed for the first several days unless it is wet, soiled, or loose, and early dressing changes are done by a trained nurse using sterile technique. No showers or baths until the team allows.</li>
<li><strong>Immobilize the catheter.</strong> Every tug tears the healing tissue at the exit site and is a recognized cause of later exit-site infection. Tape or a securement device anchors the tubing; the patient is taught never to let it hang or catch on clothing.[[ispd2023]]</li>
<li><strong>Prevent constipation and straining.</strong> A full bowel displaces the catheter tip, and straining, heavy lifting, and forceful coughing raise abdominal pressure and promote leaks and hernias. A bowel regimen is commonly prescribed from the day of insertion.</li>
<li><strong>Inspect and report.</strong> A wet dressing, bleeding, increasing pain, fever, or swelling around the site is reported the same day.</li>
<li><strong>Never use scissors near the catheter.</strong> A cut catheter is an emergency: clamp between the cut and the body and call the PD unit.</li>
</ul>
<p>Once the site has healed, exit-site care becomes the patient&rsquo;s own routine. Hand hygiene first. Look and feel along the tunnel. Clean and dry thoroughly. Apply the prescribed topical agent. Secure the catheter.[[ispd2023]]</p>
<p><strong>A catheter that never moves and a bowel that never strains give the cuffs time to seal. Protect both from the first day.</strong></p>
"""},
    {"after": "catheter", "id": "exchange", "nav": "The exchange", "tag": "Clinical application", "h2": "The exchange: the reason behind each step",
     "html": """
<p>Training teaches the steps of the system the patient uses. When you teach or audit technique, you need the reasons as well. A patient who understands why a step exists is less likely to drop it at home in the sixth month.[[figueiredo2016]]</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Principle</th><th>Reason</th><th>What to look for when observing</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Prepare the room</td><td>Moving air and animals carry organisms to an open connector</td><td>Door and windows closed, fan and air conditioner off or directed away, pets out, surface wiped, everything needed within reach before starting. Contact with household pets during exchanges is a recognized peritonitis risk[[ispd2022]]</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mask, if the program requires it</td><td>Organisms from the nose and mouth</td><td>Covers nose and mouth; anyone else in the room is masked or away from the field</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hand hygiene</td><td>Touch is the main route of peritonitis</td><td>Thorough washing and, just as important, thorough drying, or alcohol hand rub, repeated if anything non-clean is touched</td></tr>
<tr><td>Check the bag</td><td>The wrong strength changes fluid removal; a damaged bag is not sterile</td><td>Strength, volume, expiry date, clear fluid, firm squeeze for leaks, intact seals and caps. A doubtful bag is set aside, not used</td></tr>
<tr><td>Warm with dry heat</td><td>Cold fluid causes pain and shivering</td><td>A warming pad or the cycler plate; never immersion in water, which contaminates the ports. Body temperature, not hot</td></tr>
<tr><td>Connect, then drain, flush, fill</td><td>See below</td><td>Connector tips touch nothing; clamps are operated in the correct order</td></tr>
<tr><td>Inspect and measure the drain</td><td>The drained fluid is the daily test of the peritoneum</td><td>Clear enough to read print through the bag; pale yellow; volume by weight</td></tr>
<tr><td>Record</td><td>Trends guide the prescription</td><td>Date, time, bag strength, volume filled, volume drained, appearance, weight, blood pressure</td></tr>
<tr><td>Disconnect and dispose</td><td>The open transfer set is again at risk</td><td>New sterile cap applied without touching the tip; fluid emptied into the toilet; bags and lines discarded as the program advises; hands cleaned</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Why the flush comes before the fill.</strong> The moment of connection carries the greatest risk. In a twin-bag system the patient drains first. Then a small amount of fresh solution runs from the new bag straight into the drain bag, before any fluid goes toward the abdomen. Organisms introduced at the connection are carried away to the drain bag by the outgoing effluent and the flush. Only then is the drain line clamped and the abdomen filled. A patient who fills first, or skips the flush to save time, has removed the one design feature that makes the system forgiving.</p>
<p><strong>Fluid removal.</strong> Net fluid removal for an exchange is the volume drained minus the volume filled. A positive number means water was removed. A negative number means fluid was absorbed. Units differ in how they count the small overfill in each bag and the flush volume, so the patient uses the method the program taught, every time. Report values that stay negative or keep shrinking, a rising weight, or new swelling.</p>
<p><strong>Automated therapy.</strong> For APD the patient sets up the cycler in the evening. The bags are placed, the cassette and lines loaded, the machine primed, and one connection made at bedtime. The same rules about the room, the hands, and the connector tips apply, concentrated into one setup. The alarm that most often wakes patients is a drain alarm, when the machine cannot recover the expected volume. The usual causes are lying on the tubing, a kink or closed clamp, constipation, or the catheter tip resting against the pelvic wall. Teach the patient to check the line, change position, and follow the machine prompts, and to call the unit if alarms recur. Repeatedly bypassing drain alarms can leave the abdomen overfilled. Every APD household also learns manual exchanges and keeps manual supplies, so that treatment continues through a brownout or a machine fault.</p>
<p><strong>When you observe an exchange, watch the hands and the connector tips. Everything else in the room exists to protect those two seconds.</strong></p>
"""},
]

EXTRA += [
    {"after": "evidence", "id": "infection", "nav": "Infection in depth", "tag": "Evidence", "h2": "Infection in depth: exit site, tunnel, and peritonitis",
     "html": """
<p><strong>Exit-site and tunnel infection.</strong> The ISPD defines an exit-site infection by purulent discharge at the exit site, with or without redness of the surrounding skin. A tunnel infection is inflammation along the buried track: redness, swelling, tenderness, or hardening over the catheter path, or a fluid collection seen on ultrasound.[[ispd2023]] Redness alone can be early infection, a reaction to tape or cleanser, or the result of pulling on the catheter. Describe it and watch it closely. Many units record the exit site with a score that rates five features from 0 to 2: swelling, crust, redness, pain on pressure, and drainage. Purulent drainage counts as infection whatever the total. A score turns &ldquo;looks a bit red&rdquo; into something two nurses on different days can compare. When there is discharge, send a swab for culture before antibiotics start. These infections matter because the same organisms, especially <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> and <em>Pseudomonas</em>, can travel along the catheter and cause peritonitis. An infection that does not clear can cost the patient the catheter.[[ispd2023]]</p>
<p><strong>Collecting the peritonitis specimen.</strong> The first cloudy bag is the best specimen. That is why patients are told to bring it and not to discard it. If the abdomen is empty, as in an APD patient with no daytime dwell, solution is run in and left for at least 2 hours before sampling, so that the cell count can be interpreted. The effluent goes for cell count with differential, Gram stain, and culture. Inoculating fluid directly into blood-culture bottles is the preferred culture method, because it improves the chance of growing the organism.[[ispd2022]] Label the specimen with the dwell time. Collect it before the first antibiotic dose. Do not let the wait for a result delay treatment. The Gram stain is often negative in peritonitis. Its main value is early detection of yeast.</p>
<p><strong>Treatment principles.</strong> Antibiotics are preferably given into the peritoneal cavity, mixed into the dialysis bag with sterile technique, because this puts high concentrations where the infection is.[[ispd2022]] When a dose is given once daily, the bag carrying it must dwell long enough for the drug to be absorbed. The minimum dwell is in the unit protocol. Make sure the patient understands that this bag is not drained early. Drug choice and dose belong to the prescriber and are adjusted when the culture returns. The ISPD also recommends antifungal prophylaxis whenever a PD patient receives a course of antibiotics for any reason, because antibiotics clear the way for fungal peritonitis.[[ispd2022]] Pain is treated, constipation is prevented, and fibrin in cloudy fluid may call for heparin in the bag, if ordered.</p>
<p><strong>Expected course and follow-up.</strong> Pain and cloudiness should begin to improve within about two to three days. Track symptoms and bag clarity daily. Repeat effluent cell counts confirm the fall. Three situations change the goal from saving the catheter to saving the peritoneum:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Refractory peritonitis:</strong> the effluent has not cleared after 5 days of appropriate antibiotics.</li>
<li><strong>Relapsing peritonitis:</strong> a new episode within 4 weeks of finishing treatment, with the same organism or with no growth.</li>
<li><strong>Fungal peritonitis.</strong></li>
</ul>
<p>In these situations the guideline recommends removing the catheter, with temporary hemodialysis, because persisting with antibiotics risks permanent membrane damage and death.[[ispd2022]] Patients often feel this as failure. You can frame it accurately: it protects the patient and, in many cases, the chance of returning to PD later.</p>
<p><strong>Every episode is investigated.</strong> After each peritonitis the team reviews the likely cause. Was it touch contamination, an exit-site or tunnel infection, a bowel source, a recent procedure, or a change in who performs exchanges? Technique is observed again, with retraining as needed.[[ispd2022]] Keep the tone fact-finding, not blaming. Patients who fear a scolding report late.</p>
<p><strong>Unit standards.</strong> A PD program counts its infections. The ISPD 2022 targets are an overall peritonitis rate of no more than 0.40 episodes per patient-year at risk, and culture-negative peritonitis in fewer than 15% of episodes.[[ispd2022]] A high culture-negative proportion points to a specimen problem: collection after antibiotics, too small a volume, or delayed transport. The unit corrects it with the laboratory.</p>
<p><strong>Prevention around procedures.</strong> Colonoscopy and invasive gynecologic procedures can seed the peritoneum. The guideline suggests antibiotic prophylaxis beforehand and an empty abdomen during the procedure.[[ispd2022]] Your task is communication. Teach the patient to tell the PD unit about any planned procedure. Ward and endoscopy nurses check that a plan exists. Low potassium is also associated with peritonitis, and the guideline advises avoiding and treating it.[[ispd2022]]</p>
<p><strong>Specimen first, antibiotic next, cause afterward. Each step is the nurse&rsquo;s to make happen, and none waits for the one before it to be perfect.</strong></p>
"""},
    {"after": "bedside", "id": "noninfectious", "nav": "Other complications", "tag": "Clinical application", "h2": "Non-infectious complications: mechanical and metabolic",
     "html": """
<p><strong>Slow or failed drainage</strong> is the most common call a PD nurse receives. First ask whether fluid moves in neither direction, or only fails to come out. Fluid that will neither fill nor drain suggests a blockage of the tube itself: a closed clamp, a kink, or a clot or fibrin plug. Fluid that fills normally but drains poorly suggests something covering the side holes, or a tip that has moved out of the pelvis: a loaded bowel, catheter migration, or omentum wrapped around the catheter. Work from the simplest cause upward:</p>
<ol>
<li>Check that all clamps are open and trace the line for kinks, including under clothing and the dressing; lower the drain bag.</li>
<li>Change position: side to side, sitting, standing, walking.</li>
<li>Ask about bowel movements. Constipation is the most frequent cause and is treated first, with the laxative prescribed for the patient.</li>
<li>Look at the fluid for white strands of fibrin and report them; heparin added to the bag is by order.</li>
<li>If drainage is still poor, the team arranges an abdominal film to see where the tip lies, and repositioning or surgery is considered only after these steps.</li>
</ol>
<p>The patient does not keep filling an abdomen that is not draining. No one forces fluid through the catheter or pulls back hard on it.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>How it presents and why</th><th>Nursing response</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Pain on filling</td><td>Cold or fast-flowing fluid, the acidity of standard solutions, or a catheter tip pressing on tissue; air under the diaphragm causes shoulder-tip pain</td><td>Warm the bag, slow the fill, prime lines fully; persistent pain is reported, and pain with cloudy fluid is peritonitis until tested</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pain at the end of draining</td><td>Suction of the catheter tip against the pelvic lining as the abdomen empties; common in the first weeks</td><td>Reassure, treat constipation, report; the prescriber may leave a small volume behind in automated therapy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Leak around the catheter</td><td>Clear fluid wetting the dressing, usually early or after straining</td><td>Report the same day; a glucose test strip on the fluid reads high because dialysis solution is rich in glucose. Therapy is usually rested or volumes reduced</td></tr>
<tr><td>Leak into tissues</td><td>Swelling of the scrotum or labia, or a soft, thickened abdominal wall, with weight gain and low drain volumes that can be mistaken for poor fluid removal</td><td>Report; imaging locates the defect; many need surgical repair</td></tr>
<tr><td>Leak into the chest</td><td>Fluid passes through a defect in the diaphragm: breathlessness, worse lying down, low drain volumes, and a pleural effusion on one side, usually the right</td><td>Urgent report; drain the abdomen and keep the patient upright while awaiting orders</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hernia</td><td>A lump at the navel, groin, or an old scar, more obvious when standing or full</td><td>Report for surgical review. A hernia that becomes painful, hard, and cannot be pushed back, especially with vomiting, is an emergency</td></tr>
<tr><td>Blood-tinged fluid</td><td>Pink or red fluid around menstruation or ovulation, after strenuous activity or minor trauma, or soon after catheter insertion; a little blood colors a whole bag</td><td>Usually benign and clears within a few exchanges, but always reported. Heavy or persistent bleeding, pain, or dizziness needs urgent assessment</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Metabolic effects.</strong> Much of the glucose in each bag is absorbed, more from stronger bags and in fast transporters. The results are higher blood sugar and greater insulin needs in diabetes, weight gain as fat, raised triglycerides, and an early feeling of fullness that blunts appetite. Your best response is to keep the patient off strong bags in the first place, by reinforcing salt restriction, and to make sure glucose testing uses a compatible meter. <strong>Low potassium</strong> is common in PD. Potassium-free solution removes it every day, and intake may be poor. It causes weakness and cramps and slows the bowel, so the diet is often liberal in potassium, and supplements may be prescribed. <strong>Protein</strong> is lost into every drained bag, and losses rise sharply during peritonitis. The recommended intake for a stable person on PD is 1.0 to 1.2 g of protein per kilogram of body weight per day.[[nutr2020]] Many patients do not reach it without specific advice on fish, egg, chicken, and other affordable protein foods. Investigate a falling albumin. Do not just record it.</p>
<p><strong>Encapsulating peritoneal sclerosis</strong> is a rare, serious, late complication. The peritoneum thickens and wraps loops of bowel in a fibrous cocoon. It appears mainly after many years of PD, sometimes only after PD has stopped. The clues are recurrent abdominal pain, vomiting and other signs of bowel obstruction, weight loss, loss of fluid removal, and blood-stained effluent. Refer a long-term patient with these symptoms promptly to the nephrologist.</p>
<p><strong>For poor drainage, check the simple things in order before anyone reaches for an X-ray: clamps, position, bowel, fibrin.</strong></p>
"""},
]

EXTRA += [
    {"after": "noninfectious", "id": "living", "nav": "Follow-up and home", "tag": "Clinical application", "h2": "Follow-up, home logistics, and the patient on a general ward",
     "html": """
<p><strong>The clinic visit.</strong> Follow the same structure every time, so that nothing is missed:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Fluid status:</strong> weight against target, blood pressure sitting and standing, ankle and facial swelling, breathlessness, urine volume.</li>
<li><strong>The home record:</strong> bag strengths used, drain volumes, fluid removal trend, missed exchanges. Increasing use of strong bags is a finding in itself.</li>
<li><strong>The catheter:</strong> exit site and tunnel inspected and scored, catheter secured, transfer set change date.</li>
<li><strong>Technique:</strong> a periodic observed exchange, and always after peritonitis, hospitalization, or a change of operator.[[figueiredo2016]]</li>
<li><strong>Bowel habit, appetite, sleep, mood, and how the household is coping.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Laboratory results:</strong> potassium, albumin, glucose, hemoglobin, phosphate, and periodic measures of clearance and urine output as the prescriber orders.</li>
</ul>
<p>Judge adequacy from the whole picture. A patient who feels well, eats, keeps weight and albumin, has no swelling, and has controlled blood pressure is being well dialyzed. A patient who is nauseated, itchy, losing weight, or overloaded is not, whatever the clearance figure says. The ISPD recommends setting the prescription through shared decisions around the person&rsquo;s own goals. A smaller starting prescription is acceptable when remaining kidney function is good, with increases as it declines.[[brown2020]]</p>
<p><strong>Home logistics in the Philippines.</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Delivery and storage.</strong> A month of solution is heavy and bulky. Confirm the delivery schedule and who will receive and carry the boxes. Boxes are stored indoors, off the floor, away from sun, stoves, and damp walls, in their outer packaging, with the oldest stock used first. Patients on islands or in upland barangays need a larger buffer because deliveries are delayed by weather and transport.</li>
<li><strong>Typhoons, floods, and brownouts.</strong> Before the rainy season, review the plan: a reserve supply as advised by the program, kept high and in waterproof containers; hand rub, masks, caps, and a flashlight packed together; manual supplies and the skill to use them for every APD patient; the PD unit number written down, not only stored in a phone; and what to do in an evacuation center, where a screened corner, a wiped surface, and clean hands still make a safe exchange. Patients are told that a delayed or skipped exchange is less dangerous than a contaminated one, and that they should contact the unit as soon as they can.</li>
<li><strong>Travel.</strong> Supplies are counted for the trip plus spare days, exchanges are planned around stops with a clean, closed space, and the unit provides a letter and a contact at the destination where possible.</li>
<li><strong>Bathing and swimming.</strong> Advice follows the program. In general, showers are allowed once the exit site has healed, with exit-site care immediately afterward; soaking in a tub and bathing in rivers, lakes, floodwater, or public hot springs are avoided; the sea or a well-maintained pool may be permitted with a protective covering.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>The PD patient admitted to a general ward.</strong> Admission is a high-risk time, because exchanges may fall to staff who have never done one.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Notify the dialysis team on admission,</strong> whatever the reason for admission, and confirm the prescription, the supplies, and who will perform exchanges. A patient or caregiver who is well enough may continue under hospital policy. A nurse who has not been trained and assessed does not improvise.</li>
<li><strong>Do not use the catheter for anything else,</strong> and do not let exchanges lapse without a plan from the nephrologist.</li>
<li><strong>Think of peritonitis</strong> in any PD patient with abdominal pain, fever, or unexplained deterioration: look at the drained fluid.</li>
<li><strong>Medication cautions:</strong> icodextrin and glucose meters; no phosphate enemas or magnesium-containing laxatives (Module 3); care with nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, aminoglycosides, and contrast in a patient who still passes urine; antibiotics for any reason prompt the question of antifungal prophylaxis; planned bowel or gynecologic procedures are discussed with the PD team first.</li>
<li><strong>Keep the record going:</strong> weights, bag strengths, drain volumes, and appearance, in the format the PD unit uses.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Burnout.</strong> PD has no days off. After months or years, patients and especially caregivers tire. Steps are shortened, exchanges are skipped, records become implausibly regular, and peritonitis follows. Ask directly, and separately, how the patient and the caregiver are managing. A caregiver often will not admit strain in front of the patient. The team can offer options: a simpler prescription where clinically acceptable, training a second family member, assisted PD with a trained helper, a change between manual and automated therapy, or a planned transfer to hemodialysis. A planned transfer, discussed early with access prepared, is a treatment decision. An unplanned transfer through severe peritonitis and an emergency catheter is the outcome that honest conversation prevents.</p>
<p><strong>At every visit, ask who is doing the exchanges now, and how tired they are. The answer changes more than any laboratory value.</strong></p>
"""},
]
